<div class="part">Part 1 — HTTP &amp; Web (T5b)</div>

<!-- S1 -->
<div class="section" id="s1">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>1. Transport Protocol Selection Basics</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 2</span>
  </div>

  <p>This is the foundational premise running through the entire topic: <strong>the application layer chooses its own transport protocol</strong>. There is no mandate to use TCP or UDP — you could even write your own transport protocol (as long as routers will forward it and it doesn't look like a DDoS).</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Choose TCP</th><th>Choose UDP</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Data volume</td><td>Large content transfer</td><td>Short messages, single queries</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Session</td><td>Long, complex sessions</td><td>Lightweight, single request-response</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Must be guaranteed</td><td>Ask again if lost (ARQ is enough)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Server state</td><td>Needs to maintain connection state</td><td>Nearly stateless server</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Protocol design burden</td><td>High — byte stream, must define message boundaries yourself</td><td>Low</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Key Concept</div>
    TCP is a bytestream — there are no message boundaries. The application layer must define where a command starts and ends. HTTP uses a blank line <code>\n\n</code> as the command terminator, which is exactly this principle in practice.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S2 -->
<div class="section" id="s2">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>2. Web History and Origins</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 3</span>
  </div>

  <h3>The Problem with Early Internet</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Everything ran locally — if you wanted something, you downloaded it first</li>
    <li>Content discovery relied on: magazines, newsgroups, mailing lists — people shared IP addresses, not domain names</li>
    <li>Content discovery (Discovery) was a fundamental unsolved problem</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Predecessor Protocols (Archie / Gopher / WAIS) — Exam-Relevant</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Archie</strong>: A search tool for archives/repositories (Archive → Archie)</li>
    <li><strong>Gopher</strong>: Page-link browsing for content; from American slang "go for something"</li>
    <li><strong>WAIS</strong> (Wide Area Information Service): Designed by a committee/standards body; spec books were inches thick and had to be purchased; no free server implementations → nobody used it</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">Lecture Aside — Remember This</div>
    WAIS is the classic counterexample of "committee design vs. open academic work": Archie and Gopher were developed by academics and released open-source; WAIS was developed by a standards body and sold for a fee → Result: WAIS died; Archie/Gopher didn't last either; but the Web won.
  </div>

  <h3>Tim Berners-Lee and the WWW</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>IT manager at CERN (the high-energy physics lab on the French-Swiss border), 1989</li>
    <li>Constantly asked: "Where is this document? How do I get that dataset?" → Wanted to automate this</li>
    <li>Core idea came from <strong>Hypertext</strong>: text that can embed links pointing to other content</li>
    <li>Invented HTML (markup language) + HTTP (transfer protocol)</li>
    <li>First URL: <code>http://info.cern.ch/hypertext/WWW/TheProject.html</code> (CERN once shut down the server; strong public backlash led to it being restored; still accessible today)</li>
    <li>Now leads W3C (governs HTML/XML standards); HTTP is managed by IETF</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- S3 -->
<div class="section" id="s3">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>3. HTTP Version Evolution</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 4</span>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Version</th><th>Year</th><th>Key Changes</th></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP/0.9</td><td>1989</td><td>Tim's internal test version; GET only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP/1.0</td><td>RFC 1945</td><td>Official release; port 80, TCP, text protocol, one connection per resource</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP/1.1</td><td>~1997</td><td>Persistent connections, pipelining; still widely used (~30 years later)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SPDY</td><td>~2010</td><td>Google experiment; not widely adopted</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP/2.0</td><td>~2015</td><td>Request prioritization, header compression, Server Push (controversial)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HTTP/3.0</td><td>RFC 2021</td><td>Based on QUIC/UDP; built-in security; eliminates TCP head-of-line blocking</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Version Negotiation Mechanism</div>
    Every request carries a version number (e.g. <code>GET / HTTP/1.0</code>), and the server responds with its own supported version (e.g. <code>HTTP/1.1</code>), implicitly signaling "I support higher versions." All servers are backward compatible → automatic negotiation of the highest common version.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S4 -->
<div class="section" id="s4">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>4. URL Structure in Detail</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 5–7</span>
  </div>

  <pre>scheme://user:password@host:port/path?query#fragment</pre>

  <h3>Scheme (Protocol Prefix)</h3>
  <p>IANA maintains a registry; currently ~280 registered schemes. Common ones:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Scheme</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>http://</code> / <code>https://</code></td><td>Web</td><td>Default ports 80 / 443</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>callto://</code> / <code>tel://</code></td><td>Phone calls</td><td>Tapping on mobile triggers dialer directly</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>mailto://</code></td><td>Email</td><td>Can pre-fill subject / body</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>file://</code></td><td>Local files</td><td>PowerPoint doesn't recognize it (too OS-specific)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ftp://</code></td><td>File transfer</td><td>Crude: can only fetch whole files</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>User:Password (Embedded Credentials)</h3>
  <div class="callout callout-exam">
    <div class="callout-label">Security Warning — Exam Point</div>
    Username and password are written in <strong>plaintext</strong> in the URL. Even if the connection is encrypted with HTTPS, the credentials are still visible in the URL bar. Never do this.
  </div>

  <h3>Host &amp; Port</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Host: a DNS-resolvable name or an IP address</li>
    <li>If port is omitted, defaults to 80; if non-80, must be stated explicitly</li>
    <li><strong>The power of the Host Header</strong>: multiple virtual hosts can run on the same IP; the server uses the <code>Host</code> header in the HTTP request to distinguish "which website do you want?"</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Path</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Can be a real path in the filesystem</li>
    <li>Can also trigger a server-side program (e.g. a database query)</li>
    <li>The server doesn't need to tell the client whether the response came from a file or a program — as long as the format is correct</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>#Fragment (Anchor)</h3>
  <p>Jumps to a specific position within the page, e.g. <code>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_802#See_also</code>. This part is never sent to the server — it is handled entirely by the browser.</p>

  <h3>?Query (Query Parameters)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Passes parameters to server-side programs; multiple parameters joined with <code>&amp;</code></li>
    <li>Much of the "gibberish" you see in URLs is a <strong>tracking token</strong>, not a functional parameter</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">Lecture Concrete Example</div>
    Telnet to Google.com:80 and send a GET request — you get a 302 redirect; the Location points to google.com.au with <code>?gfe_rd=cr&amp;dcr=0&amp;ei=xxx</code> appended. These query params are tracking tokens letting the Australian server know "you came from our US machine," so all your search results can be traced.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S5 -->
<div class="section" id="s5">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>5. Static vs Dynamic Content</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 8</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Static Content</h3>
  <p>The server returns a file directly; content is fixed.</p>

  <h3>Server-Side Dynamic (Early: CGI)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>CGI = Common Gateway Interface: client sends a request, server runs a program and returns its output as an HTTP response</li>
    <li>Downside: 1 million simultaneous clients → server CPU melts down</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Client-Side Dynamic (Modern Mainstream)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Server pushes data (sometimes more than needed) to the client</li>
    <li>Client-side JS renders, processes, and handles interactions locally</li>
    <li>CPU load is offloaded from the server</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Combination (The Reality)</h3>
  <p>The Google Maps example:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tiles: prefetch the surrounding ring based on the viewport; dragging triggers automatic prefetch of new edges</li>
    <li>Road network overlay: a separate data layer</li>
    <li>Real-time traffic overlay: yet another layer, continuously updated</li>
    <li>Three data layers transferred independently, all running over the same HTTP connection</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Key Point</div>
    The client (browser) has no idea whether what it received came from a static file or was dynamically generated by a program — it just renders it if the format is correct. This is the core source of flexibility in Web architecture.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S6 -->
<div class="section" id="s6">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>6. The Complete HTTP 8-Step Flow</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 9</span>
  </div>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Parse the URL</strong> — extract scheme / host / port / path / query / fragment</li>
    <li><strong>DNS resolution</strong> — resolve host to an IP address</li>
    <li><strong>Establish TCP connection</strong> — three-way handshake to IP:Port</li>
    <li><strong>Send HTTP request</strong> — Method + Path + version + Headers</li>
    <li><strong>Receive content</strong> — response headers + body</li>
    <li><strong>Close TCP connection</strong> (HTTP/1.0 behavior)</li>
    <li><strong>Unpack content</strong> — decompress, decode, etc.</li>
    <li><strong>Render</strong> — browser renders HTML, triggering more GETs</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">HTTP/1.0 Performance Problem Hides Here</div>
    A webpage has 100+ resources; HTTP/1.0 repeats steps 3–6 for every resource → massive time wasted on TCP handshakes and teardowns.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S7 -->
<div class="section" id="s7">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>7. HTTP Request Methods</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 10</span>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Method</th><th>Semantics</th><th>Current Status</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>GET</code></td><td>Retrieve a resource</td><td>Most common</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>HEAD</code></td><td>Retrieve headers only, no body</td><td>Used for cache validation; saves bandwidth</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>POST</code></td><td>Submit data, append to resource</td><td>Used for form submission, interactive sites</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>PUT</code></td><td>Create/replace a file on the server</td><td>Largely deprecated — allowing remote writes is extremely risky</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DELETE</code></td><td>Delete a resource</td><td>Largely deprecated</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>LINK / UNLINK</code></td><td>Create/destroy links between resources</td><td>Obsolete</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Importance of HEAD</div>
    You can learn a resource's "last modified time" or "whether its size has changed" without downloading the whole file — this is the core mechanism behind cache validation. Far more efficient than downloading a 1MB image just to compare.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S8 -->
<div class="section" id="s8">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>8. HTTP Response Status Codes</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 11</span>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Range</th><th>Category</th><th>Typical Examples</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1xx</td><td>Informational</td><td>Rarely used in practice</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2xx</td><td>Success</td><td>200 OK; 201 Created</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3xx</td><td>Redirection</td><td>301 Moved Permanently; 302 Found/Moved Temporarily</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4xx</td><td>Client error</td><td>400 Bad Request; 403 Forbidden; 404 Not Found</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5xx</td><td>Server error</td><td>500 Internal Server Error; 503 Service Unavailable</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>301 vs 302 — Important Distinction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>301 Moved Permanently</strong>: Gone for good. The client should update its bookmarks and <em>never come back to this address</em></li>
    <li><strong>302 Moved Temporarily / Found</strong>: Temporarily away, but <em>keep coming to this address</em> in future; I'll tell you where it is right now</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-exam">
    <div class="callout-label">Exam Point — Reason Phrases Are Not Standardized</div>
    The text description (reason phrase) of a status code is <strong>not standardized</strong>. Google returns "302 Found" rather than "302 Moved Temporarily," and that's fine — the three-digit number is authoritative; the text is just a hint.
  </div>

  <h3>Practical Distinction (from Transcript)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>4xx: <strong>your fault</strong> — fix the URL or authentication</li>
    <li>5xx: <strong>server's fault</strong> — not your problem; wait and try again</li>
    <li>503 Service Unavailable: a backend process on the server has crashed</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- S9 -->
<div class="section" id="s9">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>9. Telnet Experiment Walkthrough</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 12–13</span>
  </div>

  <p>Telnet was originally a remote login protocol, but because HTTP is a plain-text protocol, Telnet can be used to manually send HTTP requests and observe protocol behavior.</p>

  <pre>telnet www.google.com 80
GET / HTTP/1.0\n\n</pre>

  <p>Note: the request must end with <strong>two newlines</strong> (one blank line) — this is HTTP's command terminator.</p>

  <h3>Normal Response (302)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Returns <code>HTTP/1.0 302 Found</code> (note: Google uses "Found" not "Moved Temporarily")</li>
    <li>Includes <code>Cache-Control: private</code>, <code>Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8</code></li>
    <li><code>Location: http://www.google.com.au/...</code> — redirects to the Australian version</li>
    <li>Body is an HTML snippet describing the redirect target</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Error Response (400)</h3>
  <pre>GET / HTTP/4.0\n\n</pre>
  <ul>
    <li>HTTP/4.0 doesn't exist → server returns <code>400 Bad Request</code></li>
    <li>Shows that version number validation is built into the protocol, not optional</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>CommBank Example (From Transcript)</h3>
  <p>Telnet to commbank.com.au:80 → immediately receives a redirect requiring a switch to HTTPS (port 443). This is the standard practice for enforcing HTTPS on banking sites.</p>
</div>

<!-- S10 -->
<div class="section" id="s10">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>10. HTTP Headers in Detail</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 14</span>
  </div>

  <p>Headers exist in both request and response directions, carrying metadata about the resource and connection.</p>

  <h3>Client → Server</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Header</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Transcript Notes</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>User-Agent</code></td><td>Browser/device info</td><td>Server can use this to serve a simplified page to mobile and a full page to desktop; differences are smaller now but still relevant</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Accept</code></td><td>Acceptable content types</td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Accept-Language</code></td><td>Preferred language list</td><td>Can say "I want Chinese / Korean / Vietnamese"; English defaults first</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Accept-Encoding</code></td><td>Supported compression formats</td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Cookie</code></td><td>Sends cookies previously set by the server</td><td>Sent automatically for the same domain</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Referer</code></td><td>Which page the link came from</td><td>Intentionally misspelled (should be "Referrer") — RFC had the typo and no one caught it before it was merged; can never be changed now; news sites rely on it to monitor traffic sources</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Authorization</code></td><td>Authentication credentials</td><td>American spelling (British is "Authorisation")</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Host</code></td><td>Target virtual host name</td><td>Essential for multiple virtual hosts on the same IP</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Range</code></td><td>Request only a byte range of the resource</td><td>Core mechanism behind video seek/scrubbing</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Server → Client</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Header</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Content-Type</code></td><td>Content type (MIME type)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Content-Length</code></td><td>Body byte count</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Content-Encoding</code></td><td>Compression format</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Content-Language</code></td><td>Language of the content</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Set-Cookie</code></td><td>Write a cookie to the client</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Location</code></td><td>Redirect target (used with 3xx)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Last-Modified</code></td><td>Time resource was last modified (for caching)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Expires</code></td><td>Cache expiry time</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ETag</code></td><td>Resource content fingerprint (similar to a checksum)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Cache-Control</code></td><td>Cache policy directives</td></tr>
  </table>
</div>

<!-- S11 -->
<div class="section" id="s11">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>11. HTTP Statelessness &amp; Cookies</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 15</span>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Key Concept</div>
    HTTP is a <strong>stateless protocol</strong>: every request is completely independent; the server saves no state for you. A TCP connection may have closed hours ago; when you make a new request the server doesn't recognize you.
  </div>

  <h3>Two Ways to Maintain a Session</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Session Token in the URL</strong> — the "gibberish" at the end of a URL, containing a timestamp (e.g. "expires in one hour")</li>
    <li><strong>Cookies</strong> — proposed by Netscape; written by the server, stored by the client, automatically sent with requests to the relevant domain</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Cookie Types</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Characteristics</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Session Cookie</td><td>Deleted when browser/tab is closed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Persistent Cookie</td><td>Has an Expiry field; persists until that date</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Secure Cookie</td><td>Transmitted only over HTTPS</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Third-party Cookie</td><td>Set by a third-party website (core tracking mechanism)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Third-Party Cookie Tracking Mechanism (Detailed in Transcript)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>You visit the ANU homepage; at the bottom of the page is a Facebook icon</li>
    <li>That icon doesn't live on ANU's server — it's a resource from Facebook's server → your browser fetches it from Facebook</li>
    <li>Facebook returns the icon and sets a cookie on your browser</li>
    <li>You visit the Canberra Times; same Facebook icon appears; your browser requests it again, sending that same cookie</li>
    <li>Facebook now knows: this user visited both ANU and the Canberra Times</li>
    <li>After accumulating enough sites, Facebook has a detailed picture of your browsing behavior</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">The 1×1 Pixel Tracking Trick</div>
    Some sites embed a 1×1 white transparent image sourced from a third-party server. You can't see it, but your browser must request it to render the page → the third-party server seizes the chance to set a cookie and track you.
  </div>

  <p>Transcript suggestion: check your browser's cookie list — after a week of normal browsing you might have <strong>thousands</strong>.</p>
</div>

<!-- S12 -->
<div class="section" id="s12">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>12. Protocol Performance Issues</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 16–17</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Why Performance Matters</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>1-second slower page load → users go buy from a competitor</li>
    <li>Modern web pages have 100+ individual resources: HTML, JS, CSS, fonts, images, iframes…</li>
    <li>The actual content (HTML text) is a tiny fraction of total data (ANU homepage HTML is a small blue block; images are a large purple block)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sources of Performance Bottlenecks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Processing capacity of browser and server</li>
    <li>Content structure and complexity</li>
    <li>HTTP protocol itself (connection management)</li>
    <li>TCP protocol behavior (congestion control — covered in Topic 7)</li>
    <li>Network path, bandwidth, RTT</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Three Improvement Approaches</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Adapt content to client capabilities (smaller images for small screens)</li>
    <li>Caching — avoid re-downloading the same content</li>
    <li>Improve HTTP connection management</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<!-- S13 -->
<div class="section" id="s13">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>13. Persistent Connections, Pipelining &amp; Parallel Requests</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 18–21</span>
  </div>

  <h3>HTTP/1.0 — Original Mode (Slow)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Each resource: TCP open → HTTP GET → download → TCP close</li>
    <li>Every TCP handshake/teardown is pure wasted time</li>
    <li>The fraction of time actually transferring data is tiny (most time spent handshaking and waiting)</li>
    <li>Gets worse the more resources a page has</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Approach 1: Parallelism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Open 8+ TCP connections simultaneously and GET in parallel</li>
    <li>Pro: no server-side changes needed</li>
    <li>Cons: bursty CPU/NIC load; bursty traffic → higher packet loss; 8× resource cost on both ends; 100 resources on ANU homepage → open 100 connections?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Approach 2: Persistent Connection — HTTP/1.1</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Open one TCP connection, request multiple resources sequentially, close when done</li>
    <li>Eliminates repeated handshake/teardown overhead</li>
    <li>Still sequential: must wait for each response before sending the next request</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Approach 3: Pipelining — HTTP/1.1</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>While downloading HTML, parse it; as soon as you discover you need an image, immediately send a GET without waiting for the previous one to finish</li>
    <li>Server processes requests in order and returns responses in order</li>
    <li>Problem 1: <strong>Head-of-Line (HOL) Blocking</strong> — one slow resource (database query, damaged disk) blocks everything behind it</li>
    <li>Problem 2: Impact of TCP congestion control (detailed in Topic 7)</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">What Modern Browsers Actually Do</div>
    A mix of all three: persistent connections + pipelining + limited parallelism. Timeout on a slow resource triggers a retry. No single approach is perfect.
  </div>

  <h3>Reading the Comparison Diagram (Slide 21)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Yellow = TCP open; Green = HTTP request; Blue = data transfer; Red = TCP close</li>
    <li>Original: every resource has a full yellow-green-blue-red cycle with lots of idle time</li>
    <li>Persistent: removes the repeated yellow/red in the middle; total time noticeably shorter</li>
    <li>Persistent + Pipeline: requests are sent earlier; blue blocks are more compact, but still constrained by TCP and HOL</li>
  </ul>
</div>

