<!-- S15 -->
<div class="section" id="s15">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>15. Caching Mechanisms</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 24 / 28</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Browser Local Cache</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sites you've visited once you'll probably visit again; the browser optimistically stores everything</li>
    <li>Must validate whether a resource has changed — cannot blindly use the old version</li>
    <li>The browser tracks which sites you visit most frequently and prioritizes their cache</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Cache Validation Mechanisms (Built into HTTP)</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>HTTP Version</th><th>How It Works</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Expires</code></td><td>1.0</td><td>Server tells you "use the cache until this date"</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Last-Modified</code></td><td>1.0</td><td>Last modification time; client can use <code>If-Modified-Since</code> conditional GET to validate</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ETag</code></td><td>1.1</td><td>Like a content fingerprint/checksum; client uses <code>If-None-Match</code> to validate</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>HEAD</code> request</td><td>1.0</td><td>Fetch headers only, not body; used for quick "has it changed?" check</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Partial GET (HTTP/1.1)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Range header: request only a specific byte range of a resource</li>
    <li>Most common use: video player requests frames for a specific time window; YouTube's "grey progress bar" prefetch works this way</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Original Design Intent</div>
    Tim Berners-Lee built caching into HTTP when he designed it in 1989 — it wasn't bolted on later; it's a core design feature of the protocol.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S16 -->
<div class="section" id="s16">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>16. Proxies and Caching Proxies</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 25–27</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Proxy Basics</h3>
  <p>A proxy sits between the client and server and does work on the client's behalf. To the client, the proxy looks like a server; to the server, the proxy looks like a client — it <strong>splits one TCP connection into two</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Benefits of a Caching Proxy</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Beneficiary</th><th>Specific Benefit</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Client</td><td>Closer cache → lower latency</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Network / ISP</td><td>Reduced egress bandwidth (content hits inside campus/corporate network)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Server</td><td>Fewer requests, lower load</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Organizational security</td><td>Can perform security scanning here (check file types, executable content)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Access control</td><td>Organization can enforce access policies (block certain content types)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Limitations of Caching Proxies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Encrypted traffic (HTTPS) cannot be read → cannot be cached</li>
    <li>Dynamic content (same URL but different database results) cannot be cached</li>
    <li>"One-off" content occupies cache space (one student looked up something nobody else will ever search)</li>
    <li>VPN traffic bypasses the proxy</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Proxy-Specific HTTP Headers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Proxy-Authenticate</code>: proxy authentication</li>
    <li><code>Forwarded</code>: forwarding info (original client IP)</li>
    <li><code>Proxy-Connection</code>: proxy connection control</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How Does the Client Know Which Proxy to Use?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Manually configure proxy address in the browser</li>
    <li>Automatic proxy discovery (PAC file)</li>
    <li>Network admin performs <strong>Deep Packet Inspection (DPI)</strong>: inspects HTTP payload of every TCP flow and transparently intercepts → extremely expensive but some do it</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">Security Trap</div>
    If you think you're connected to the proxy via HTTPS but the proxy forwards traffic to the outside in plain HTTP, you think you're secure but you're not. The proxy has stripped your encryption and is forwarding unencrypted.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S17 -->
<div class="section" id="s17">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>17. CDN (Content Delivery Network)</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 29</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Difference from Caching Proxies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Caching proxy: passive — only fills on client request</li>
    <li>CDN: proactive — <strong>pushes content to cache nodes worldwide before requests arrive</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How CDNs Work</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Content provider (e.g. CNN) contracts with a CDN</li>
    <li>CDN deploys cache nodes globally; content is pushed there in advance</li>
    <li>DNS is configured: user queries cnn.com → DNS returns the nearest cache node IP</li>
    <li>Resource links in HTML also point to cache nodes (not the origin server)</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>The Akamai Story (Detailed in Transcript)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Founded 1995–1996; saw the opportunity back in the HTTP/1.0 era</li>
    <li>Hundreds of PoPs (Points of Presence) worldwide, seeing massive global Web traffic</li>
    <li>Deep insight into network behavior and attack patterns</li>
    <li>Still an extremely profitable and effective commercial company today</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Win:Win:Win</div>
    Users win (faster), content providers win (less server load), ISPs win (less backbone traffic). All three benefit — that's the fundamental reason CDNs succeeded.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- S18 -->
<div class="section" id="s18">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>18. HTTP 2.0 / 3.0 / QUIC</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 30–32</span>
  </div>

  <h3>HTTP/2.0</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Clients can set <strong>priorities</strong> on server requests (send HTML/CSS first, images can wait)</li>
    <li><strong>Header compression</strong> (HPACK)</li>
    <li><strong>Server Push</strong>: server predicts what you'll need next and proactively sends it ("you want the homepage, you'll definitely want this logo too")</li>
    <li>Server Push is highly controversial and complex to implement; HTTP/2.1 hasn't appeared yet</li>
    <li>Apache and other servers added some extra optimizations in their HTTP/2 implementations but it's somewhat messy</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>HTTP/3 / QUIC</h3>

  <div class="callout callout-exam">
    <div class="callout-label">High-Priority Exam Area</div>
    HTTP/3 is based on QUIC; QUIC is based on UDP but adds some TCP functionality. The fundamental goal is to eliminate TCP's head-of-line blocking and reduce the number of RTTs needed to establish a connection.
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Details</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Base protocol</td><td>UDP (Quick UDP Internet Connections)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Self-implemented at the UDP layer: sequence numbers, checksums (similar to TCP but more flexible)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Security</td><td>Built-in TLS, completed during connection setup, <strong>reduced from 7 RTTs to 2</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>HOL blocking</td><td>Each stream is independent; a packet loss in one stream doesn't block others</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Compatibility</td><td>Some ISPs rate-limit UDP → automatic fallback to TCP</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Standardization</td><td>RFC 8999–9002 (2021)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>QUIC Development History (From Transcript)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Google started developing around 2011–2012</li>
    <li><strong>By 2013–2014 it was quietly deployed in Chrome and Google's servers</strong> — global users became unwitting test subjects</li>
    <li>Researchers captured it in Wireshark and asked "what protocol is this?"</li>
    <li>IETF was unhappy about this development approach but eventually accepted and pushed standardization forward</li>
    <li>About 1/3 of HTTP traffic now runs on QUIC</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Who Uses HTTP/3</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Browsers: Chrome, Edge (Chromium), Firefox, Safari, Opera</li>
    <li>Mobile: Android native library, iOS</li>
    <li>Servers: NGINX, LiteSpeed, MS IIS ✓ — <strong>Apache: not yet</strong></li>
    <li>Commercial: Google, Facebook, Instagram, Uber</li>
    <li>Detection tool: <code>http3check.net</code></li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- S19 -->
<div class="section" id="s19">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>19. HTTP Abused as a "Transport Protocol"</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 33</span>
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li>HTTP now carries real-time audio/video (scenarios that should use RTP)</li>
    <li>SOAP, REST, AJAX — machine-to-machine RPC (remote procedure calls) running over HTTP</li>
    <li>Used for firewall traversal: everything is blocked by the firewall, but the CEO needs internet access → ports 80/443 can't be blocked → everyone stuffs their traffic into HTTP</li>
    <li>One reason Chrome has grown so large: HTTP carries more and more functionality that doesn't belong to it</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">Lecture Commentary</div>
    Using HTTP to bypass firewalls is "a bit unfair, but that's the reality" — not the optimal solution, but the universal one.
  </div>
</div>

