<div class="part">Part 2 — Real-Time Communication (T5c)</div>

<!-- R1 -->
<div class="section" id="r1">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>1. Challenges of Real-Time Traffic</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 3–4</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Applications That Need "Real-Time"</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Audio/video: phone calls, video conferencing (bidirectional interactive)</li>
    <li>Remote surgery / telemedicine</li>
    <li>Robot control, industrial control (valves, signals, railway switches)</li>
    <li>VR/AR: phone moves fast; overlay must align in real-time; high latency causes misalignment</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-exam">
    <div class="callout-label">Core Trade-off — Runs Throughout This Topic</div>
    <strong>Timeliness (low latency)</strong> vs <strong>Reliability (no loss)</strong> — you cannot have both. Reliability requires retransmission; retransmission takes time. You must choose a balance point for your application.
  </div>

  <h3>Complexity of Real-Time Communication</h3>
  <p>A video conference with N sites has <strong>O(N²)</strong> complexity — every pair of sites needs to maintain connections and synchronization.</p>

  <h3>Extreme Cases from the Transcript</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Remote music ensemble: stage in Europe + cellist in Argentina + dancer in Thailand, performing live together. The musicians' brains adapted to the latency and played slightly ahead of the beat</li>
    <li>Hawaiian doctor performing surgery in Thailand: sends "scalpel forward," then "stop" — if the "stop" message is lost, the consequences are catastrophic</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- R2 -->
<div class="section" id="r2">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>2. Real-Time Requirements &amp; Application Differences</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 5</span>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Application Type</th><th>Direction</th><th>Recommended Protocol</th><th>Latency Tolerance</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Video streaming (YouTube)</td><td>Unidirectional</td><td>TCP / HTTP</td><td>Several seconds of buffering acceptable</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Video conferencing (Zoom)</td><td>Bidirectional interactive</td><td>UDP</td><td>Very low; ideally under 150ms</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Robot control</td><td>Bidirectional</td><td>UDP (or dedicated network)</td><td>Very low, and high reliability also required</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Human Perception Characteristics (Transcript)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Video: can tolerate 90% packet loss; the brain compensates for blur/freeze</li>
    <li>Audio: 10% loss makes it essentially unusable</li>
    <li>Audio-video lip sync: if audio and video delays are inconsistent, that's worse than simple latency</li>
    <li>International satellite phone example: ~2 second round trip between Australia and Europe; conversation becomes "taking turns monologuing" rather than an actual dialogue</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- R3 -->
<div class="section" id="r3">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>3. Components of Network Delay</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 6–9</span>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">The Internet Is Best-Effort</div>
    The internet guarantees nothing unless you have a dedicated circuit. Routers, paths, bandwidth — none of it belongs to you; you cannot control it.
  </div>

  <h3>Components of Delay</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Component</th><th>Description</th><th>Controllable?</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Propagation delay (latency)</td><td>Speed-of-light limit; pure physics</td><td>No — cannot be overcome</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Queuing delay</td><td>Waiting in router queues</td><td>No (you don't own the routers)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Jitter (PDV)</td><td>Random variation in delay</td><td>Can be mitigated at the receiver with a buffer</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Ideal vs Reality (Slides 8–9 Illustration)</h3>
  <p>Ideal: all packets arrive with a constant delay (like circuit switching; delay distribution is a single spike)</p>
  <p>Reality:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Some packets took the fastest path and arrive early (left tail)</li>
    <li>Most packets arrive near the "typical delay" (distribution peak)</li>
    <li>Some packets were rerouted, congested, or fragmented and arrive very late (right tail)</li>
    <li>The "typical delay" is higher than the physical minimum; the spread around this peak is jitter</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sender vs Receiver Timing (Slides 11–12)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sender: packets 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 sent at evenly-spaced clock intervals</li>
    <li>Receiver: packet 1 on time; packet 2 slightly late; packet 3 faster than 2; packet 4 very late; packet 5 normal…</li>
    <li>Arrival order may also be shuffled: 1, 3, 2, 5, 4</li>
    <li>The receiver must handle both reordering and delay</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- R4 -->
<div class="section" id="r4">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>4. Jitter (Packet Delay Variation) — Measured</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 10</span>
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li>Typical jitter is ~15–17ms; won't drop below 15ms (from Slide 10 UDP traffic measurement)</li>
    <li>15ms ≈ the duration of one frame at 60fps (1/60s ≈ 16.7ms)</li>
    <li>Occasional spikes can exceed 30ms — equivalent to 2 frames of uncertainty</li>
    <li>Jitter itself varies over time; it's not static</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">Why Jitter Is More Problematic Than Latency</div>
    Fixed latency can be compensated by pre-buffering (wait long enough, then play). But jitter is random — you don't know how late the next packet will be → how large should the buffer be? This is the core design problem of real-time communication.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- R5 -->
<div class="section" id="r5">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>5. Receiver Buffer Mechanism</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 13–15</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Why Buffering Is Needed</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sender: emits audio/video samples at a fixed rate</li>
    <li>Receiver: also needs to play them out at a fixed rate</li>
    <li>The network introduces random delays and reordering → a buffer is needed to "smooth things out"</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How the Buffer Works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Receive Buffer</strong>: collects packets and reorders them</li>
    <li><strong>Playout Buffer</strong>: holds them in the correct order and releases them at timed intervals</li>
    <li>Buffer size is measured in bytes, but what it really represents is a "time window"</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Maximum Acceptable Delay (Slide 15)</h3>
  <p>Set a threshold: packets that haven't arrived by this deadline are treated as "lost" — no more waiting. This threshold is your Receive Buffer size (in the time dimension).</p>

  <p>Example from Slide 14: packet 4 arrives at the very last moment, already past the playout buffer's time window → the application treats it as "lost" (even though it physically arrived).</p>
</div>

<!-- R6 -->
<div class="section" id="r6">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>6. Buffer Size Trade-offs</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 16</span>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Large Buffer</th><th>Small Buffer</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Jitter tolerance</td><td>High (can afford to wait)</td><td>Low (can't afford to wait)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>End-to-end latency</td><td>High (wait to fill up before playing)</td><td>Low (near real-time)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Packet loss rate</td><td>Low (fewer packets time out)</td><td>High (more packets time out)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Unidirectional streaming, recorded video</td><td>Video conferencing, real-time control</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Design Principle</div>
    The smaller the buffer, the better the real-time performance, but the more likely you are to "glitch." Video can tolerate occasional glitches; audio almost cannot. Each application weighs this for itself — there's no universal answer.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- R7 -->
<div class="section" id="r7">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>7. Loss Recovery Techniques</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 17 / 20</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Retransmission — Rarely Used</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Detect lost packet → ask the sender to retransmit</li>
    <li>Cost: round-trip RTT + processing time + re-queuing delay</li>
    <li>Means the Receive Buffer must be very large (wait for the retransmitted packet)</li>
    <li>Sender must also buffer already-sent data (in case retransmission is needed)</li>
    <li>Exception: in <strong>IP Multicast</strong>, you can request retransmission from "the nearest neighbor" (see next section)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Forward Error Correction (FEC)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Sender embeds redundant information in the data</li>
    <li>Receiver can reconstruct lost/corrupted packets without retransmission</li>
    <li>Useful for: control signal reliability; scenarios where retransmission cost is too high (e.g. deep-space probes)</li>
    <li>Also called "Adaptive FEC" — dynamically adjusts redundancy based on network conditions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Codec Adaptation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>High packet loss rate → reduce bitrate/resolution, send less data</li>
    <li>Requires a back-channel (receiver informs sender of network conditions)</li>
    <li>YouTube automatically dropping from 1080p to 360p is this mechanism</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Parallel Transmission</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>When sending frame N, also include a compressed low-quality copy of frame N-1</li>
    <li>If frame N-1 is lost, frame N contains a backup</li>
    <li>Cost: more bandwidth; Benefit: low-quality fill-in when there is loss</li>
    <li>Also applies to control signal reliability (Slide 20 diagram: 4 3 2 1 sent in parallel)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Error Concealment (Interpolation)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Interpolate between frames; approximate a lost frame using neighboring frames</li>
    <li>Similar in concept to progressive image compression</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- R8 -->
<div class="section" id="r8">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>8. IP Multicast</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 18</span>
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li>Sender sends one copy; the network automatically replicates it at branch points to multiple receivers</li>
    <li>All receivers can also be senders (Everyone is a listener AND a sender)</li>
    <li>Loss recovery: receivers don't need to request retransmission from the original sender; they can ask <strong>the nearest neighbor</strong> (whoever has the packet sends it)</li>
    <li>Significantly reduces retransmission latency (neighbor is closer than the source)</li>
    <li>Used for large-scale distribution (corporate intranet video broadcasts, etc.)</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- R9 -->
<div class="section" id="r9">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>9. Elastic Buffer / Adaptive Playout</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 19</span>
  </div>

  <h3>How It Works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Monitor packet loss rate: normal 1/100 → suddenly 4/100 → network has degraded</li>
    <li>Expand the buffer: wait a bit longer; give packets more time to arrive</li>
    <li><strong>Slow down playout</strong>: user sees the video slow down slightly</li>
    <li>When network recovers: shrink the buffer; <strong>speed up playout to catch up</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-tip">
    <div class="callout-label">What's Really Happening in Zoom/Teams Stutters</div>
    When you see someone suddenly in "slow motion" and then "speed up" to catch back up — that's the elastic buffer expanding and contracting. It slows playback when expanding and fast-forwards to catch up when shrinking; typically lasts 0.5–1 second.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- R10 -->
<div class="section" id="r10">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>10. RTP — Real-Time Transport Protocol</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 21</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Basic Role</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Runs on top of UDP (can also run over TCP)</li>
    <li>Supports any media encoding format</li>
    <li>Supports multiplexing multiple media streams (e.g. audio stream + video stream in the same conference)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Key Header Fields</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Sequence Number</td><td>Packet sequence number; used for detecting loss and reordering</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Timestamp</td><td>Media timestamp; used for synchronizing playback (not a network timestamp)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SSRC (Synchronisation Source Identifier)</td><td>Identifies the stream source; used for lip-sync (aligning audio and video)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CSRC (Contributing Source Identifier)</td><td>Identifies the original sources in a mixed stream</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Payload Type</td><td>Media encoding type (for selecting a decoder)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>M (Marker bit)</td><td>Marks important events (e.g. frame boundaries)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>RTP Capabilities</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Multiplex multiple audio/video streams; select language/subtitles</li>
    <li>Lip synchronization</li>
    <li>Can carry non-standard media like whiteboards and shared documents</li>
    <li>Supports real-time translated subtitle overlays (mentioned in Transcript)</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- R11 -->
<div class="section" id="r11">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>11. RTCP — RTP Control Protocol</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 22</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Design Purpose</h3>
  <p>The sender needs to know: how is my stream's quality? Should I adjust bitrate, FEC strength, or retransmission strategy?</p>

  <h3>Core Mechanism</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Uses <strong>odd-numbered ports</strong> (RTP uses even ports); out-of-band control that doesn't interfere with the media stream</li>
    <li>Must limit RTCP bandwidth usage (can't let control traffic eat into media bandwidth)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Main Report Types</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Type</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Sender Report</td><td>Sender statistics: how many packets/bytes sent, NTP timestamp</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Receiver Report</td><td>Receiver statistics: packet loss rate, highest sequence number, jitter, RTT</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Source Description</td><td>Identifies participant information</td></tr>
    <tr><td>BYE</td><td>Leaving-session notification</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Heartbeat</td><td>I'm still alive; the stream is still flowing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Retransmission Request</td><td>Request retransmission of a specific packet</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-key">
    <div class="callout-label">Value of RTCP</div>
    Sender receives "0% packet loss" → increase video quality; receives "10% packet loss" → reduce bitrate or strengthen FEC. This is the core feedback loop for real-time adaptation.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- R12 -->
<div class="section" id="r12">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>12. Complete Video Conferencing Protocol Stack</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 23</span>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
    <tr><td>SIP</td><td>Establishing/terminating calls ("making/hanging up a call")</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SDP</td><td>Negotiating media parameters (codecs, resolution, bandwidth caps)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RTP</td><td>Transporting the actual audio/video data</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RTCP</td><td>Transporting control/statistics; drives adaptation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Receiver buffer</td><td>Handles jitter and reordering; drives elastic playout</td></tr>
  </table>
</div>

<!-- R13 -->
<div class="section" id="r13">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>13. SIP — Session Initiation Protocol</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 24–25</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Role</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>IETF open standard (RFC 3261+); not concerned with the actual media transport mechanism</li>
    <li>Primarily used for VoIP / enterprise phone systems (widely used after Australia's NBN rollout)</li>
    <li>Components: Proxy, Registrar, Redirect Server, Border Controller, Gateway (for PSTN access)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Protocol Appearance</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Looks like HTTP: commands, responses, status codes (but no XYZ codes)</li>
    <li>Runs over both TCP and UDP</li>
    <li>Signaling flow: INVITE → 180 Ringing → 200 OK → ACK → [RTP media] → BYE → 200 OK</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>SIP vs H.323</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th></th><th>SIP</th><th>H.323 (ITU)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Origin</td><td>IETF / academic</td><td>Telecom standards body (telecoms ministry ecosystem)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Specification</td><td>Open and free</td><td>Inches-thick spec; costs thousands of dollars to buy</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Adoption</td><td>Widespread (VoIP)</td><td>Only survives in some enterprise video conferencing systems</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">SIP's Awkward Position</div>
    SIP is an excellent open standard, but Zoom, WhatsApp, Telegram, FaceTime, Teams, Messenger — <strong>none of them use SIP</strong>; all use proprietary protocols. Commercial interests keep each platform locked in its own ecosystem. Skype has also disappeared by now.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- R14 -->
<div class="section" id="r14">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>14. Unicast Streaming &amp; RTSP</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slides 26–27</span>
  </div>

  <h3>Unicast Streaming vs Video Conferencing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Unicast streaming: doesn't care about latency (a few seconds of buffer is fine); emphasis on smooth and reliable delivery</li>
    <li>Receiver "pulls" content (rather than sender "pushing")</li>
    <li>Can use TCP: reliable transport; sliding window manages playout buffer</li>
    <li>Progress bar: red = played, grey = pre-fetched, white/empty = not yet downloaded</li>
    <li>Seeking into the grey area: plays immediately; seeking into white: pause and wait for download</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>RTSP (Real Time Streaming Protocol)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Establishes streaming sessions and negotiates transport parameters (can use RTP/RTCP or HTTP, etc.)</li>
    <li>Commands: <code>OPTIONS</code>, <code>DESCRIBE</code>, <code>SETUP</code>, <code>PLAY</code> (with time range), <code>PAUSE</code>…</li>
    <li>Looks like HTTP and SIP</li>
    <li>YouTube/Netflix don't use RTSP; they use proprietary solutions</li>
    <li><strong>Most common real-world use: security cameras</strong> — most IP cameras provide an RTSP interface</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout-tip">
    <div class="callout-label">Practical Tip (Transcript)</div>
    RTSP streams from security cameras can be accessed and recorded directly via command-line clients (e.g. ffmpeg, VLC); you can also seek between frames.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- R15 -->
<div class="section" id="r15">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>15. DASH / HTTP Streaming</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 28</span>
  </div>

  <h3>DASH (Dynamic Adaptive Streaming over HTTP)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Implements adaptive streaming over HTTP</li>
    <li>Uses <code>HEAD</code> requests to discover media info and available formats</li>
    <li>Uses <code>GET</code> + <code>Range</code> to download video segments and fill the playout buffer</li>
    <li>Server can perform adaptive encoding on request (different bitrates/resolutions)</li>
    <li>HTML5 built-in <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> tag (the killer of Flash)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why Use HTTP for Streaming</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Firewall traversal: HTTP ports are almost never blocked</li>
    <li><strong>Stateless server</strong> (each GET is independent) → much easier to scale</li>
    <li>Inherits HTTP's proxy, caching, and CDN infrastructure naturally</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>YouTube Traffic Characteristics (Transcript)</h3>
  <p>Capturing YouTube traffic in Wireshark shows a <strong>sawtooth pattern</strong>: the client periodically downloads several MB to fill the buffer, then pauses while playback consumes it, then downloads again. This is DASH's rhythm.</p>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <div class="callout-label">"Sigh, Let's Just Use HTTP" (Slide 28 Title)</div>
    RTSP/RTP are more purpose-built streaming protocols, but YouTube and Netflix chose HTTP/DASH for blunt reasons: firewalls, existing infrastructure, and simpler development. Professional doesn't always win — pragmatic does.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- R16 -->
<div class="section" id="r16">
  <div class="section-header">
    <h2>16. Summary</h2>
    <span class="slide-ref">Slide 29</span>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-exam">
    <div class="callout-label">Final Conclusion — Lecture's Own Words</div>
    "Achieving perfect, low-latency real-time transmission over a best-effort network is genuinely hard. All the intelligence lives at the endpoints; the network itself guarantees nothing."
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li>All optimizations for real-time communication (elastic buffer, FEC, codec adaptation) are implemented at the <strong>receiver/sender endpoints</strong></li>
    <li>The network just does its best to forward packets — no QoS guaranteed (unless you have a dedicated circuit: MPLS, RSVP, QoS)</li>
    <li>Audio/video "sort of works" — under your network conditions</li>
    <li>Robots/industrial control using real-time traffic: the internet may not be the best choice, but sometimes it's the only choice</li>
    <li>The human brain is highly adaptive (musicians adapted to the latency); robots cannot self-adapt</li>
  </ul>
</div>

